(window.RK_DATA = window.RK_DATA || {})["pro-en"] = {"modules":[{"id":"a00","title":"Bridge from the basics","sub":"A quick check that everything is in place before Level 2","lessons":[{"id":"a00-1","title":"How Level 2 works","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Welcome to Level 2","md":"You finished the basics. Now we go **deeper**: how traits, iterators, threads, async, macros and `unsafe` really work.\n\nSame rules as before:\n- One lesson, one idea.\n- Short steps. Press \"Next\" at your own pace.\n- \"Deep dive\" cards can be skipped. They are for people who want to know how things work inside.\n\n> **Analogy:** Level 1 taught you to drive. Level 2 opens the hood so you can see the engine."},{"t":"text","title":"What is a bit different","md":"- Examples are longer. Sometimes 20–30 lines. Read them top to bottom, one piece at a time.\n- Sometimes we use **crates** (other people's libraries): `tokio`, `serde`, `rayon`. Rust Playground already has them, nothing to install.\n- More tasks where you write a piece of a real program, not just print a line."},{"t":"text","title":"If it gets hard","md":"That's normal. Level 2 topics are harder. What helps:\n- Go back to the Level 1 lesson on the same topic. The text will point to it.\n- Take a break and do the lesson tomorrow. Your progress is saved.\n- Run the example and change one thing in it. See what changes."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What do you do with a \"Deep dive\" card if it doesn't interest you right now?","options":["You must read it, or you can't go on","Press \"Next\" and skip it","Start the lesson over"],"answer":1,"why":["No, it is optional.","Right. You can come back to it later.","No need."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Level 2 has the same format, deeper topics.","Crates are already in the Playground.","If it's hard, go back to the basics or take a break."]}],"goals":["See how Level 2 differs from Level 1","Know what to do if it gets hard"]},{"id":"a00-2","title":"Review: ownership and borrowing","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Three ownership rules","md":"1. Every value has an **owner** — one variable.\n2. There can be only **one** owner.\n3. When the owner goes out of scope, the value is dropped.\n\nIf you forgot the details, see the Level 1 lesson \"Ownership\"."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let a = String::from(\"cat\");\n    let b = a; // ownership moved to b\n    println!(\"{b}\");\n}","out":"cat","after":"After `let b = a;` you can't use `a` anymore. Try adding `println!(\"{a}\");` and run it."},{"t":"text","title":"Borrowing","md":"- `&x` — let someone **look**. You can have as many of these as you like.\n- `&mut x` — let someone **change** it. There can be only **one** such reference, and while it exists there are no others.\n\n> **Analogy:** many people can read a library book. But you can only fix it when it's in your hands alone."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How many `&mut` references to one value can exist at the same time?","options":["As many as you like","One","Zero, you can't create them"],"answer":1,"why":["That's `&`.","Right. And while it lives, there are no plain `&` either.","You can, if the variable is declared with `mut`."]},{"t":"fill","explain":"To change someone else's value, the function takes `&mut String`, and the call passes `&mut word`.","md":"Fill in the blanks so the function changes the string and `main` prints it.","code":"fn shout(s: ___ String) {\n    s.push('!');\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut word = String::from(\"yay\");\n    shout(___ word);\n    println!(\"{word}\");\n}","answers":[["&mut"],["&mut"]]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `longest` must return the length of the longest string in the list. It must not take the list: after the call, `main` prints it again.","code":"fn longest(words: Vec<String>) -> usize {\n    let mut best = 0;\n    for w in words {\n        if w.len() > best {\n            best = w.len();\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let words = vec![String::from(\"cat\"), String::from(\"hello\"), String::from(\"Rust\")];\n    println!(\"{}\", longest(words));\n    println!(\"{}\", words.len());\n}","solution":"fn longest(words: &[String]) -> usize {\n    let mut best = 0;\n    for w in words {\n        if w.len() > best {\n            best = w.len();\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let words = vec![String::from(\"cat\"), String::from(\"hello\"), String::from(\"Rust\")];\n    println!(\"{}\", longest(&words));\n    println!(\"{}\", words.len());\n}","expect":"5\n3","hints":["Let the function take a reference: `words: &[String]`, and call it as `longest(&words)`.","`len()` counts bytes. For English letters that's the same as the number of letters."]},{"t":"recap","items":["One owner. Pass it on and the value moves.","`&` — look (many), `&mut` — change (one).","Functions usually get a reference, not the value itself."]}],"goals":["Recall the three ownership rules","Recall the difference between `&` and `&mut`"]},{"id":"a00-3","title":"Review: enum, Result, traits","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"enum and match","md":"`enum` means \"one of several\". `match` handles every variant.\n\n```\nenum Light { Red, Yellow, Green }\n\nfn action(l: Light) -> &'static str {\n    match l {\n        Light::Red => \"stop\",\n        Light::Yellow => \"wait\",\n        Light::Green => \"go\",\n    }\n}\n```\n`match` must cover **all** variants. Forget one and the compiler reminds you."},{"t":"text","title":"Result and ?","md":"`Result<T, E>` is either `Ok(value)` or `Err(error)`.\n\nA `?` after a call means: \"if it's an error, return it upward right away; if it's a success, take the value out\"."},{"t":"code","code":"fn double(text: &str) -> Result<i32, std::num::ParseIntError> {\n    let n: i32 = text.parse()?;\n    Ok(n * 2)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", double(\"21\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", double(\"cat\").is_err());\n}","out":"Ok(42)\ntrue"},{"t":"text","title":"A trait is a skill","md":"A trait describes **what a type can do**. For example, `Display` means \"I can be printed with `{}`\".\n\n```\ntrait Speak {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n```\nAny type can implement a trait with `impl Speak for Type`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `?` after `text.parse()` do?","options":["Prints the error and goes on","On error, leaves the function and returns it; on success, gives the value","Turns the error into 0"],"answer":1,"why":["No, it returns the error from the function.","Right.","That's what `unwrap_or(0)` does."]},{"t":"task","md":"Implement the `Speak` trait for `Cat` and `Dog`. The cat says `Meow`, the dog says `Woof`.","code":"trait Speak {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat;\nstruct Dog;\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Cat.speak());\n    println!(\"{}\", Dog.speak());\n}","solution":"trait Speak {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat;\nstruct Dog;\n\nimpl Speak for Cat {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String {\n        String::from(\"Meow\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Speak for Dog {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String {\n        String::from(\"Woof\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Cat.speak());\n    println!(\"{}\", Dog.speak());\n}","expect":"Meow\nWoof","hints":["Start like this: `impl Speak for Cat { fn speak(&self) -> String { ... } }`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`enum` + `match` — handle every variant.","`Result` + `?` — errors travel upward by themselves.","A trait is a set of skills; `impl Trait for Type` teaches a type."]}],"goals":["Recall `match` on an `enum`","Recall `Result` and `?`","Recall what a trait is"]},{"id":"a00-p","title":"Practice: warm-up","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Find the first number greater than 10 in the list and print it. If there is none, print `none`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let nums = vec![3, 8, 15, 4, 22];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let nums = vec![3, 8, 15, 4, 22];\n    match nums.iter().find(|&&n| n > 10) {\n        Some(n) => println!(\"{n}\"),\n        None => println!(\"none\"),\n    }\n}","expect":"15","hints":["Iterators have a `find` method. It returns an `Option`.","`nums.iter().find(|&&n| n > 10)` — double `&`, because `find` gives a reference to a reference."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `Item` struct holds a name and a price in cents. Add up the cart and print the total in dollars with two digits: `Total: 377.50`.","code":"struct Item {\n    name: String,\n    cents: u32,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let cart = vec![\n        Item { name: String::from(\"bread\"), cents: 5500 },\n        Item { name: String::from(\"cheese\"), cents: 25000 },\n        Item { name: String::from(\"tea\"), cents: 7250 },\n    ];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"struct Item {\n    name: String,\n    cents: u32,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let cart = vec![\n        Item { name: String::from(\"bread\"), cents: 5500 },\n        Item { name: String::from(\"cheese\"), cents: 25000 },\n        Item { name: String::from(\"tea\"), cents: 7250 },\n    ];\n    let total: u32 = cart.iter().map(|i| i.cents).sum();\n    println!(\"Total: {}.{:02}\", total / 100, total % 100);\n}","expect":"Total: 377.50","hints":["`cart.iter().map(|i| i.cents).sum()` gives the sum. Write the type: `let total: u32 = ...`.","Dollars are `total / 100`, cents are `total % 100`. `{:02}` adds a leading zero: `5` → `05`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `parse_age` turns text into an age. An empty string is the error `\"empty\"`, not a number is the error `\"not a number\"`. Use `?` or `match`.","code":"fn parse_age(text: &str) -> Result<u32, String> {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"30\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"thirty\"));\n}","solution":"fn parse_age(text: &str) -> Result<u32, String> {\n    if text.is_empty() {\n        return Err(String::from(\"empty\"));\n    }\n    text.parse::<u32>().map_err(|_| String::from(\"not a number\"))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"30\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_age(\"thirty\"));\n}","expect":"Ok(30)\nErr(\"empty\")\nErr(\"not a number\")","hints":["First check `text.is_empty()` and return `Err(...)`.","`map_err(|_| ...)` swaps one error for another."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`find` looks for the first match and returns an `Option`.","`map` + `sum` give a total in one line.","`map_err` changes the error type."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Warm up before Level 2","Combine structs, `Vec`, `Option` and iterators"]}]},{"id":"a01","title":"Traits, deeper","sub":"Default methods, dyn, associated types, operators","lessons":[{"id":"a01-1","title":"Default methods and traits on top of traits","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Default method","md":"A trait can not only declare a method but also **write its body right away**. Then a type doesn't have to implement it.\n\n```\ntrait Greet {\n    fn name(&self) -> String;          // required\n    fn hello(&self) -> String {        // default\n        format!(\"Hello, {}!\", self.name())\n    }\n}\n```\nThe type implements only `name` and gets `hello` for free.\n\n> **Analogy:** a letter template. You fill in the name, the rest of the text is already there."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Greet {\n    fn name(&self) -> String;\n    fn hello(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"Hello, {}!\", self.name())\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Cat;\nstruct Robot;\n\nimpl Greet for Cat {\n    fn name(&self) -> String { String::from(\"Kitty\") }\n}\n\nimpl Greet for Robot {\n    fn name(&self) -> String { String::from(\"R2\") }\n    fn hello(&self) -> String { String::from(\"BEEP-BOOP\") } // its own\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Cat.hello());\n    println!(\"{}\", Robot.hello());\n}","out":"Hello, Kitty!\nBEEP-BOOP","after":"`Robot` replaced the default method with its own. That's allowed too."},{"t":"quiz","md":"A type implements a trait but doesn't write a method that has a default body. What happens?","options":["Compile error","The type gets the ready-made version","The method will be empty"],"answer":1,"why":["No, that's what the default body is for.","Right.","It does what the trait says."]},{"t":"text","title":"A trait on top of a trait","md":"Sometimes a trait needs another trait. For example: \"to be able to `Report`, a type must be printable with `{}`\".\n\n```\nuse std::fmt::Display;\n\ntrait Report: Display {\n    fn report(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"[report] {}\", self)\n    }\n}\n```\n`trait Report: Display` reads \"Report requires Display\". Such a \"parent\" trait is called a **supertrait**."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::fmt;\n\ntrait Report: fmt::Display {\n    fn report(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"[report] {}\", self)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Temp(i32);\n\nimpl fmt::Display for Temp {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"{}°C\", self.0)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Report for Temp {}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Temp(21).report());\n}","out":"[report] 21°C","after":"`impl Report for Temp {}` is empty: everything has a default. Remove `impl fmt::Display` and see the error."},{"t":"task","md":"Add a default method `describe` to the `Shape` trait that returns `area: X`, where X is the result of `area()`. The square implements only `area`.","code":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32;\n    // add describe\n}\n\nstruct Square(u32);\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.0 * self.0 }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Square(4).describe());\n}","solution":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32;\n    fn describe(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"area: {}\", self.area())\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Square(u32);\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.0 * self.0 }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Square(4).describe());\n}","expect":"area: 16","hints":["A default method can call other trait methods: `self.area()`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"The orphan rule","md":"You can implement a trait for a type only if **the trait or the type is yours** (declared in your crate).\n\nYou can't write `impl Display for Vec<i32>`: both `Display` and `Vec` belong to someone else. Otherwise two crates could do it in different ways, and Rust wouldn't know whose version wins.\n\nThis is the **orphan rule**. The usual way around it is a wrapper: `struct MyList(Vec<i32>);` is your own type, and you can do anything with it."},{"t":"recap","items":["A method with a body in a trait is a default method. It can be overridden.","`trait A: B` — to be `A`, a type must be `B`.","You can't implement someone else's trait for someone else's type."]}],"goals":["Give a trait method a ready-made body","Require one trait for another"]},{"id":"a01-2","title":"Associated types","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A type inside a trait","md":"Remember iterators? The `Iterator` trait has this line:\n\n```\ntrait Iterator {\n    type Item;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;\n}\n```\n`type Item;` is an **associated type**. Each iterator says **what** it yields: numbers, strings, anything.\n\n> **Analogy:** a vending machine. The trait says \"I can hand out a product\". Which product is up to each machine: one sells chocolate, another sells water."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Container {\n    type Item;\n    fn get(&self, i: usize) -> Option<&Self::Item>;\n    fn first(&self) -> Option<&Self::Item> {\n        self.get(0)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Shelf {\n    books: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nimpl Container for Shelf {\n    type Item = String;\n    fn get(&self, i: usize) -> Option<&String> {\n        self.books.get(i)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let s = Shelf { books: vec![String::from(\"Dune\"), String::from(\"The Hobbit\")] };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", s.first());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", s.get(5));\n}","out":"Some(\"Dune\")\nNone","after":"`type Item = String;` — the shelf holds strings."},{"t":"text","title":"Why not a generic?","md":"You could write `trait Container<T>` instead. The difference:\n- `Container<T>` — a type can implement it **many times**: for `T = i32`, for `T = String`…\n- `type Item` — **exactly once**. A shelf holds one kind of thing.\n\nIf the answer is \"one type per implementation\", use an associated type. The code is simpler: no `<T>` everywhere."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How many times can one type implement a trait with an associated type `type Item`?","options":["Once","Any number of times, with different `Item`s","Never, such traits exist only in the standard library"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. And it has one `Item`.","That's how a generic trait `Trait<T>` works.","You can write your own."]},{"t":"fill","explain":"The associated type of `Iterator` is called `Item`, and the value is returned inside `Some(...)`.","md":"Fill in the blanks: the counter yields `u32` numbers.","code":"struct Counter { n: u32 }\n\nimpl Iterator for Counter {\n    type ___ = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {\n        if self.n < 3 {\n            self.n += 1;\n            ___(self.n)\n        } else {\n            None\n        }\n    }\n}","answers":[["Item"],["Some"]]},{"t":"recap","items":["`type Item;` in a trait is a type that each implementation chooses.","The implementation writes `type Item = Concrete;`.","One type, one implementation, one `Item`."]}],"goals":["Understand `type Item` inside a trait","Write a trait with your own associated type"]},{"id":"a01-3","title":"impl Trait and dyn Trait","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Problem: different types in one list","md":"A `Vec` holds values of **one** type. What if you want a list of shapes: circles, squares, triangles?\n\n```\nlet shapes = vec![Circle, Square]; // error: the types differ\n```\nThe fix is `Box<dyn Shape>`: \"a box with something that can do Shape\"."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64;\n    fn name(&self) -> &str;\n}\n\nstruct Circle { r: f64 }\nstruct Square { side: f64 }\n\nimpl Shape for Circle {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 { 3.14 * self.r * self.r }\n    fn name(&self) -> &str { \"circle\" }\n}\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 { self.side * self.side }\n    fn name(&self) -> &str { \"square\" }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> = vec![\n        Box::new(Circle { r: 1.0 }),\n        Box::new(Square { side: 2.0 }),\n    ];\n    for s in &shapes {\n        println!(\"{}: {:.2}\", s.name(), s.area());\n    }\n}","out":"circle: 3.14\nsquare: 4.00"},{"t":"text","title":"Two ways to say \"any Shape\"","md":"- `impl Shape` — \"some **one** concrete type, the compiler knows which\". Fast: the compiler makes a separate copy of the code for each type.\n- `dyn Shape` — \"**any** type, we'll find out while running\". Needed when types are mixed. A bit slower: the method is looked up in a table.\n\n> **Analogy:** `impl` is a pizza order where you know in advance which pizza arrives. `dyn` is a surprise box: open it and see."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\n// impl in an argument: any type with Display fits\nfn show(x: impl Display) {\n    println!(\"<{x}>\");\n}\n\n// impl in a return type: \"I return an iterator, which one doesn't matter\"\nfn evens(max: u32) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32> {\n    (0..=max).filter(|n| n % 2 == 0)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    show(5);\n    show(\"cat\");\n    let v: Vec<u32> = evens(6).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","out":"<5>\n<cat>\n[0, 2, 4, 6]","after":"`impl Iterator<Item = u32>` as a return type is very common: the real iterator type is long and scary, and this way you don't have to write it."},{"t":"quiz","md":"You need a `Vec` holding both `Circle` and `Square`. What do you pick?","options":["`Vec<impl Shape>`","`Vec<Box<dyn Shape>>`","`Vec<Shape>`"],"answer":1,"why":["`impl` means one concrete type, you can't mix.","Right. `dyn` lets you store different types.","That won't compile: you need `dyn` and a box."]},{"t":"text","title":"Why a box is needed","md":"A `dyn Shape` might be a circle (8 bytes), a square (8 bytes) or something of 100 bytes. Its size is **unknown** in advance, but a `Vec` wants equal slots.\n\n`Box` fixes that: the slot holds only a pointer (known size), and the shape itself lives on the heap. A reference works too: `&dyn Shape`."},{"t":"deep","title":"How dyn works: the method table","md":"A `Box<dyn Shape>` actually holds **two** pointers:\n1. to the data (the circle or square itself);\n2. to a **vtable** — this type's table of methods: \"here is `area`, here is `name`\".\n\nWhen you call `s.area()`, the program looks in the table and jumps to the right function. This is called **dynamic dispatch**.\n\nWith `impl` (and generics `<T: Shape>`) the compiler knows the type in advance and calls the function directly. This is **static dispatch**: faster, but the code is copied for every type.\n\nNot every trait can be used as `dyn`. For example, if a method returns `Self` or is generic itself, the table can't be built. Such traits are not **dyn compatible**."},{"t":"task","mustContain":["dyn Animal"],"md":"Make a list of animals `Vec<Box<dyn Animal>>` with a cat and a dog, and print their voices in a loop.","code":"trait Animal {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat;\nstruct Dog;\n\nimpl Animal for Cat {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String { String::from(\"meow\") }\n}\n\nimpl Animal for Dog {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String { String::from(\"woof\") }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    // your code\n}","solution":"trait Animal {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat;\nstruct Dog;\n\nimpl Animal for Cat {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String { String::from(\"meow\") }\n}\n\nimpl Animal for Dog {\n    fn voice(&self) -> String { String::from(\"woof\") }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let zoo: Vec<Box<dyn Animal>> = vec![Box::new(Cat), Box::new(Dog)];\n    for a in &zoo {\n        println!(\"{}\", a.voice());\n    }\n}","expect":"meow\nwoof","hints":["The list type is `Vec<Box<dyn Animal>>`. The items are `Box::new(Cat)`, `Box::new(Dog)`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`impl Trait` — one concrete type known to the compiler. Fast.","`dyn Trait` — any type, found out while running. Needed for mixed lists.","`dyn` lives behind a pointer: `Box<dyn T>` or `&dyn T`."]}],"goals":["See the difference between `impl Trait` and `dyn Trait`","Put different types into one `Vec`"]},{"id":"a01-4","title":"Operators and From","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Operators are traits too","md":"When you write `a + b`, Rust calls the `add` method of the `std::ops::Add` trait. Implement `Add` for your type and `+` works.\n\n> **Analogy:** the `+` sign is a button. The `Add` trait is the instruction for what happens when you press it."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::ops::Add;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nimpl Add for Point {\n    type Output = Point;\n    fn add(self, other: Point) -> Point {\n        Point { x: self.x + other.x, y: self.y + other.y }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n    let b = Point { x: 10, y: 20 };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", a + b);\n}","out":"Point { x: 11, y: 22 }","after":"See `type Output`? That's an associated type from the last lesson: \"what you get from adding\"."},{"t":"text","title":"Other operators","md":"They all live in `std::ops`:\n- `Sub` — `-`, `Mul` — `*`, `Div` — `/`\n- `Neg` — a minus in front: `-a`\n- `AddAssign` — `+=`\n- `Index` — square brackets: `a[i]`\n\nComparisons `==` and `<` are `PartialEq` and `PartialOrd`. You usually get them with `#[derive]`."},{"t":"text","title":"From and into","md":"`From` is the trait \"how to make my type from another one\".\n\n```\nstruct Meters(f64);\n\nimpl From<f64> for Meters {\n    fn from(x: f64) -> Self { Meters(x) }\n}\n```\nBonus: implement `From<A> for B` and `A` gets an `.into()` method that turns it into `B`."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Celsius(f64);\nstruct Fahrenheit(f64);\n\nimpl From<Celsius> for Fahrenheit {\n    fn from(c: Celsius) -> Self {\n        Fahrenheit(c.0 * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let f = Fahrenheit::from(Celsius(100.0));\n    println!(\"{}\", f.0);\n    let g: Fahrenheit = Celsius(0.0).into();\n    println!(\"{}\", g.0);\n}","out":"212\n32","after":"With `into()` you must say **what** you convert into: `let g: Fahrenheit = ...`. Otherwise the compiler can't tell."},{"t":"quiz","md":"You implemented `impl From<u8> for Level`. What else appeared by itself?","options":["Nothing","An `.into()` method on `u8` that makes a `Level`","`From<Level> for u8`"],"answer":1,"why":["Something useful did.","Right. `Into` comes from `From` for free.","The reverse conversion must be written separately."]},{"t":"order","explain":"First the `use`, then `impl Mul<u32> for Money`, inside it the result type and the method itself.","md":"Put together an implementation of `Mul` for `Money` so that `Money(5) * 3` gives `Money(15)`.","lines":["use std::ops::Mul;","impl Mul<u32> for Money {","    type Output = Money;","    fn mul(self, k: u32) -> Money {","        Money(self.0 * k)","    }","}"]},{"t":"task","md":"Implement `Sub` for `Point` so that `b - a` works.","code":"use std::ops::Sub;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n    let b = Point { x: 10, y: 20 };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", b - a);\n}","solution":"use std::ops::Sub;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nimpl Sub for Point {\n    type Output = Point;\n    fn sub(self, other: Point) -> Point {\n        Point { x: self.x - other.x, y: self.y - other.y }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n    let b = Point { x: 10, y: 20 };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", b - a);\n}","expect":"Point { x: 9, y: 18 }","hints":["Same as `Add`, only the method is called `sub`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Operators are traits from `std::ops`: `Add`, `Sub`, `Mul`…","`From<A> for B` — how to make a `B` from an `A`. `.into()` appears by itself.","Give `into()` the result type."]}],"goals":["Make your type work with `+`","Convert types with `From` and `into()`"]},{"id":"a01-p","title":"Practice: traits","min":15,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"The `Priced` trait has a `price()` method in cents and a default method `with_tax()` that adds 20%. Implement `Priced` for `Book` (price 50000) and print both prices.","code":"trait Priced {\n    fn price(&self) -> u32;\n    fn with_tax(&self) -> u32 {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Book;\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let b = Book;\n    println!(\"{}\", b.price());\n    println!(\"{}\", b.with_tax());\n}","solution":"trait Priced {\n    fn price(&self) -> u32;\n    fn with_tax(&self) -> u32 {\n        self.price() * 120 / 100\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Book;\n\nimpl Priced for Book {\n    fn price(&self) -> u32 { 50000 }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let b = Book;\n    println!(\"{}\", b.price());\n    println!(\"{}\", b.with_tax());\n}","expect":"50000\n60000","hints":["Adding 20% means multiplying by 120 and dividing by 100."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `total_area` takes a slice of shapes `&[Box<dyn Shape>]` and returns the sum of their areas. Write it.","code":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32;\n}\n\nstruct Rect { w: u32, h: u32 }\nstruct Sq(u32);\n\nimpl Shape for Rect {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.w * self.h }\n}\nimpl Shape for Sq {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.0 * self.0 }\n}\n\nfn total_area(shapes: &[Box<dyn Shape>]) -> u32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> = vec![\n        Box::new(Rect { w: 2, h: 3 }),\n        Box::new(Sq(4)),\n        Box::new(Sq(1)),\n    ];\n    println!(\"{}\", total_area(&shapes));\n}","solution":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32;\n}\n\nstruct Rect { w: u32, h: u32 }\nstruct Sq(u32);\n\nimpl Shape for Rect {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.w * self.h }\n}\nimpl Shape for Sq {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 { self.0 * self.0 }\n}\n\nfn total_area(shapes: &[Box<dyn Shape>]) -> u32 {\n    shapes.iter().map(|s| s.area()).sum()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> = vec![\n        Box::new(Rect { w: 2, h: 3 }),\n        Box::new(Sq(4)),\n        Box::new(Sq(1)),\n    ];\n    println!(\"{}\", total_area(&shapes));\n}","expect":"23","hints":["`shapes.iter().map(|s| s.area()).sum()`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write `impl From<&str> for Color`: `\"red\"` → `Color::Red`, `\"green\"` → `Color::Green`, anything else → `Color::Unknown`. Then call `.into()`.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Color {\n    Red,\n    Green,\n    Unknown,\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Color = \"red\".into();\n    let b: Color = \"green\".into();\n    let c: Color = \"purple\".into();\n    println!(\"{a:?} {b:?} {c:?}\");\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Color {\n    Red,\n    Green,\n    Unknown,\n}\n\nimpl From<&str> for Color {\n    fn from(s: &str) -> Self {\n        match s {\n            \"red\" => Color::Red,\n            \"green\" => Color::Green,\n            _ => Color::Unknown,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Color = \"red\".into();\n    let b: Color = \"green\".into();\n    let c: Color = \"purple\".into();\n    println!(\"{a:?} {b:?} {c:?}\");\n}","expect":"Red Green Unknown","hints":["Inside `from`, write `match s { \"red\" => ..., \"green\" => ..., _ => ... }`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `Describe` trait requires `Debug` (a supertrait). Its default method `describe` returns `This is: ` and the debug print of the value. Finish the trait and implement it for `Pet`.","code":"use std::fmt::Debug;\n\ntrait Describe {\n    fn describe(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"This is: {:?}\", self)\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Pet {\n    name: String,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Pet { name: String::from(\"Bagel\") };\n    println!(\"{}\", p.describe());\n}","solution":"use std::fmt::Debug;\n\ntrait Describe: Debug {\n    fn describe(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"This is: {:?}\", self)\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Pet {\n    name: String,\n}\n\nimpl Describe for Pet {}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Pet { name: String::from(\"Bagel\") };\n    println!(\"{}\", p.describe());\n}","expect":"This is: Pet { name: \"Bagel\" }","hints":["Right now the trait doesn't know that `self` can be printed with `{:?}`. Write `trait Describe: Debug`.","The implementation can be empty: `impl Describe for Pet {}`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Default methods save code.","`dyn` puts different types into one list.","`From` does conversions, and `.into()` comes as a bonus."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Practice default methods, `dyn` and `From`"]}]},{"id":"a02","title":"Iterators from the inside","sub":"Your own iterator, IntoIterator, fold, collect into anything","lessons":[{"id":"a02-1","title":"Your own iterator","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"An iterator is just next","md":"To become an iterator, a type implements **one** method:\n\n```\nfn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>\n```\n- More values left — return `Some(value)`.\n- Out of values — return `None`.\n\nEverything else (`map`, `filter`, `sum`, `collect`…) is a default method. They appear by themselves.\n\n> **Analogy:** a napkin dispenser. Pull and you get a napkin. Empty, and nothing comes out."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Countdown {\n    n: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Countdown {\n    type Item = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        if self.n == 0 {\n            None\n        } else {\n            self.n -= 1;\n            Some(self.n + 1)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for x in (Countdown { n: 3 }) {\n        println!(\"{x}\");\n    }\n    println!(\"liftoff!\");\n}","out":"3\n2\n1\nliftoff!","after":"Why `&mut self`? The iterator **remembers where it stopped** and updates that state on every step."},{"t":"code","md":"Your own iterator can already do everything normal ones can:","code":"struct Countdown {\n    n: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Countdown {\n    type Item = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        if self.n == 0 {\n            None\n        } else {\n            self.n -= 1;\n            Some(self.n + 1)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let total: u32 = Countdown { n: 5 }.filter(|x| x % 2 == 1).sum();\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","out":"9","after":"5 + 3 + 1 = 9. We wrote only `next`, and `filter` and `sum` came for free."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which method must you implement for a type to become an iterator?","options":["`iter`","`next`","`map` and `filter`"],"answer":1,"why":["That's a collection method, not an iterator one.","Right. Everything else is a default.","They are already in the trait."]},{"t":"text","title":"Endless iterators","md":"`next` may **never** return `None`. That's fine — just take as many as you need with `take`.\n\n```\nstruct Fib { a: u64, b: u64 }\n\nimpl Iterator for Fib {\n    type Item = u64;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u64> {\n        let r = self.a;\n        self.a = self.b;\n        self.b += r;\n        Some(r)\n    }\n}\n```\n`Fib { a: 0, b: 1 }.take(10)` gives the first 10 Fibonacci numbers."},{"t":"task","md":"Finish the `Fib` iterator and print the first 8 Fibonacci numbers on one line by collecting into a `Vec`.","code":"struct Fib {\n    a: u64,\n    b: u64,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Fib {\n    type Item = u64;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u64> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<u64> = Fib { a: 0, b: 1 }.take(8).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"struct Fib {\n    a: u64,\n    b: u64,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Fib {\n    type Item = u64;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u64> {\n        let r = self.a;\n        self.a = self.b;\n        self.b += r;\n        Some(r)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<u64> = Fib { a: 0, b: 1 }.take(8).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13]","hints":["Remember the current `a`, then shift: `a` becomes `b`, and `b` becomes the sum of the old ones.","`let r = self.a; self.a = self.b; self.b += r; Some(r)`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Where the state lives","md":"Every iterator has its own state: a counter, a position in an array, the last two Fibonacci numbers.\n\nWhen you write `v.iter().map(f).filter(g)`, you get a **nesting doll** of structs: `Filter<Map<Iter<i32>, F>, G>`. Each one holds the inner one and its own closure. Calling `next` on the outer one calls `next` on the inner one.\n\nThe compiler can flatten this doll into a simple loop. That's why an iterator chain in Rust is usually as fast as a hand-written `for` loop. This is called a **zero-cost abstraction**."},{"t":"recap","items":["Iterator = `type Item` + `fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Item>`.","`None` means the end. You may never end and use `take(n)`.","All adapters (`map`, `filter`, `sum`…) come for free."]}],"goals":["Write a type that can do `next()`","Get every iterator method for free"]},{"id":"a02-2","title":"IntoIterator: how for works","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"What for really does","md":"The line `for x in collection` turns into roughly this:\n\n```\nlet mut it = collection.into_iter();\nwhile let Some(x) = it.next() {\n    // loop body\n}\n```\n`into_iter()` is a method of the `IntoIterator` trait: \"how to make an iterator out of me\"."},{"t":"text","title":"Three ways to walk a Vec","md":"- `for x in v.iter()` (or `for x in &v`) — `x` is `&T`. We look.\n- `for x in v.iter_mut()` (or `for x in &mut v`) — `x` is `&mut T`. We change.\n- `for x in v` (or `v.into_iter()`) — `x` is `T`. We take. After the loop `v` is gone.\n\n> **Analogy:** a box of candy. Look at each one, rearrange each one, or eat them all. After the third, the box is empty."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    for x in &mut v {\n        *x *= 10;\n    }\n    for x in &v {\n        print!(\"{x} \");\n    }\n    println!();\n    let total: i32 = v.into_iter().sum();\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","out":"10 20 30\n60","after":"`*x *= 10` — the star is needed because `x` is a reference here, and we change the number itself."},{"t":"quiz","md":"After `for s in names { ... }` where `names: Vec<String>` — can you still use `names`?","options":["Yes, the loop changes nothing","No, a by-value `for` took the vector","Yes, but only `names.len()`"],"answer":1,"why":["The loop took ownership.","Right. Write `for s in &names` if you still need the vector.","Not even that."]},{"t":"text","title":"Your own type in for","md":"Want to write `for x in &my_list`? Implement `IntoIterator` for `&MyList`. The easiest way is to hand out the inner vector's iterator:\n\n```\nstruct Playlist { songs: Vec<String> }\n\nimpl<'a> IntoIterator for &'a Playlist {\n    type Item = &'a String;\n    type IntoIter = std::slice::Iter<'a, String>;\n    fn into_iter(self) -> Self::IntoIter {\n        self.songs.iter()\n    }\n}\n```\n`'a` is a lifetime: the items live as long as the playlist. More in the lifetimes module."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Playlist {\n    songs: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> IntoIterator for &'a Playlist {\n    type Item = &'a String;\n    type IntoIter = std::slice::Iter<'a, String>;\n    fn into_iter(self) -> Self::IntoIter {\n        self.songs.iter()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Playlist { songs: vec![String::from(\"Intro\"), String::from(\"Outro\")] };\n    for s in &p {\n        println!(\"♪ {s}\");\n    }\n    println!(\"total: {}\", p.songs.len());\n}","out":"♪ Intro\n♪ Outro\ntotal: 2"},{"t":"fill","explain":"`iter_mut()` gives `&mut i32`, and `*x` is the number itself that we change.","md":"Fill in the blanks: double each number in place, then print the vector.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    for x in v.___() {\n        ___x *= 2;\n    }\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","answers":[["iter_mut"],["*"]]},{"t":"recap","items":["`for x in c` = `c.into_iter()` + `next()` in a loop.","`&v` → `&T`, `&mut v` → `&mut T`, `v` → `T` (the vector is eaten).","Your type in `for` — implement `IntoIterator`."]}],"goals":["See that `for` calls `into_iter()`","Tell apart `iter()`, `iter_mut()`, `into_iter()`"]},{"id":"a02-3","title":"Adapters people forget","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Pair up and join","md":"- `a.zip(b)` — walk two iterators **in pairs**: `(a1, b1)`, `(a2, b2)`… It ends when the shorter one ends.\n- `a.chain(b)` — all of `a`, then all of `b`.\n- `enumerate()` — add a number: `(0, x)`, `(1, y)`…"},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let names = [\"Ann\", \"Bob\", \"Vicky\"];\n    let scores = [90, 75, 82];\n    for (n, s) in names.iter().zip(scores.iter()) {\n        println!(\"{n}: {s}\");\n    }\n    let all: Vec<i32> = (1..3).chain(7..9).collect();\n    println!(\"{all:?}\");\n}","out":"Ann: 90\nBob: 75\nVicky: 82\n[1, 2, 7, 8]"},{"t":"text","title":"Windows and chunks (on slices)","md":"- `v.windows(2)` — a sliding window: `[a,b]`, `[b,c]`, `[c,d]`…\n- `v.chunks(2)` — slicing without overlap: `[a,b]`, `[c,d]`, `[e]`.\n\n> **Analogy:** `windows` is looking out of a train window: each frame is a bit shifted. `chunks` is slicing a loaf of bread."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let temps = [3, 5, 4, 8, 10];\n    let rises = temps.windows(2).filter(|w| w[1] > w[0]).count();\n    println!(\"it got warmer {rises} times\");\n\n    for part in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].chunks(2) {\n        println!(\"{part:?}\");\n    }\n}","out":"it got warmer 3 times\n[1, 2]\n[3, 4]\n[5]"},{"t":"text","title":"Stop and skip","md":"- `take_while(condition)` — take **while** the condition is true. First \"false\" — stop.\n- `skip_while(condition)` — skip while true, then take everything.\n- `step_by(n)` — every n-th one.\n- `rev()` — backwards."},{"t":"text","title":"Unfold nested things","md":"`flat_map` is like `map`, but each item turns into **several**, and everything is glued into one stream.\n\n```\nlet words = [\"cat\", \"dog\"];\nlet letters: Vec<char> = words.iter().flat_map(|w| w.chars()).collect();\n// ['c', 'a', 't', 'd', 'o', 'g']\n```\nIf the items are already iterators or `Vec`s, `flatten()` is enough."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `[1, 2, 3, 10, 4].iter().take_while(|&&x| x < 5).count()` return?","options":["4","3","5"],"answer":1,"why":["`take_while` stops at the first non-match, and the `4` after `10` isn't counted.","Right. `1, 2, 3`, then `10` — stop.","Not all items are below 5."]},{"t":"task","md":"Count how many times two neighboring numbers in the list are **equal**. Use `windows(2)`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let v = [1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let v = [1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4];\n    let pairs = v.windows(2).filter(|w| w[0] == w[1]).count();\n    println!(\"{pairs}\");\n}","expect":"3","hints":["`v.windows(2)` gives slices of two items: `w[0]` and `w[1]`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`zip` — in pairs, `chain` — one after another, `enumerate` — with a number.","`windows` — a sliding window, `chunks` — pieces.","`take_while`, `skip_while`, `step_by`, `rev` — steer the stream.","`flat_map`/`flatten` — unfold nested things."]}],"goals":["Meet `zip`, `chain`, `windows`, `flat_map`, `take_while`","Build short pipelines instead of loops"]},{"id":"a02-4","title":"fold and collect into anything","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"fold — roll everything into one","md":"`fold(start, |accumulated, x| new_accumulated)`.\n\n```\nlet sum = [1, 2, 3].iter().fold(0, |acc, x| acc + x); // 6\n```\n`sum`, `count`, `max` are special cases of `fold`. When there's no ready-made method, use `fold`.\n\n> **Analogy:** a snowball. Roll it through the snow and with every step it picks up a bit more."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let words = [\"rust\", \"is\", \"delightful\"];\n    let longest = words.iter().fold(\"\", |best, &w| {\n        if w.chars().count() > best.chars().count() { w } else { best }\n    });\n    println!(\"{longest}\");\n\n    let sentence = words.iter().fold(String::new(), |mut s, w| {\n        if !s.is_empty() { s.push(' '); }\n        s.push_str(w);\n        s\n    });\n    println!(\"{sentence}\");\n}","out":"delightful\nrust is delightful"},{"t":"text","title":"collect can do a lot","md":"`collect()` looks at the **type** you ask for and builds it:\n- `Vec<T>` — a list.\n- `String` — from chars or strings.\n- `HashMap<K, V>` or `BTreeMap<K, V>` — from `(key, value)` pairs.\n- `Result<Vec<T>, E>` — from a stream of `Result`s. The first error makes the whole result `Err`."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let s: String = ['R', 'u', 's', 't'].iter().collect();\n    println!(\"{s}\");\n\n    let ages: BTreeMap<&str, u32> = [(\"Ann\", 30), (\"Bob\", 25)].into_iter().collect();\n    println!(\"{ages:?}\");\n\n    let good: Result<Vec<i32>, _> = [\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"].iter().map(|x| x.parse::<i32>()).collect();\n    println!(\"{good:?}\");\n\n    let bad: Result<Vec<i32>, _> = [\"1\", \"cat\", \"3\"].iter().map(|x| x.parse::<i32>()).collect();\n    println!(\"{}\", bad.is_err());\n}","out":"Rust\n{\"Ann\": 30, \"Bob\": 25}\nOk([1, 2, 3])\ntrue","after":"`BTreeMap` keeps keys in order. So the output is always the same — handy for examples."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What do you get if you collect `[\"4\", \"x\", \"6\"]` into `Result<Vec<i32>, _>` with `parse`?","options":["`Ok([4, 6])`","`Err(...)`","`Ok([4, 0, 6])`"],"answer":1,"why":["Errors aren't silently dropped.","Right. One error and the whole result is an error.","Rust doesn't put in a zero by itself."]},{"t":"text","title":"peekable — peek at the next one","md":"Sometimes you need to look at the next item **without taking** it. That's `peekable()`:\n\n```\nlet mut it = [1, 2, 3].iter().peekable();\nif let Some(&&next) = it.peek() {\n    // next == 1, but it's still in the iterator\n}\n```\nUseful when parsing text: \"is the next char a digit? then keep reading the number\"."},{"t":"task","md":"Count how many times each word appears. Collect into a `BTreeMap<&str, u32>` and print it.","code":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"cat dog cat fish cat dog\";\n    let mut counts: BTreeMap<&str, u32> = BTreeMap::new();\n    // your code\n    println!(\"{counts:?}\");\n}","solution":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"cat dog cat fish cat dog\";\n    let mut counts: BTreeMap<&str, u32> = BTreeMap::new();\n    for w in text.split_whitespace() {\n        *counts.entry(w).or_insert(0) += 1;\n    }\n    println!(\"{counts:?}\");\n}","expect":"{\"cat\": 3, \"dog\": 2, \"fish\": 1}","hints":["`text.split_whitespace()` gives the words.","`*counts.entry(w).or_insert(0) += 1;` — bump the word's counter, creating it the first time."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`fold(start, |acc, x| ...)` — roll into one value.","`collect` builds the type you asked for.","`Result<Vec<_>, _>` via `collect` — stop at the first error.","`peekable` — look at the next one without taking it."]}],"goals":["Fold a stream into one value with `fold`","Collect into `String`, `HashMap`, `Result<Vec>`"]},{"id":"a02-p","title":"Practice: iterators","min":15,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"The `Steps` iterator yields numbers from `start` to `end` inclusive with a `step`. Finish `next`.","code":"struct Steps {\n    cur: u32,\n    end: u32,\n    step: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Steps {\n    type Item = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<u32> = Steps { cur: 2, end: 11, step: 3 }.collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"struct Steps {\n    cur: u32,\n    end: u32,\n    step: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Steps {\n    type Item = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        if self.cur > self.end {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let r = self.cur;\n        self.cur += self.step;\n        Some(r)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<u32> = Steps { cur: 2, end: 11, step: 3 }.collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[2, 5, 8, 11]","hints":["If `cur` is already past `end` — `None`. Otherwise remember `cur`, add `step` and return the remembered value."]},{"t":"task","md":"You have names and scores. Print only those above 80, as `name — score`. Use `zip` and `filter`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let names = [\"Ann\", \"Bob\", \"Vicky\", \"Greg\"];\n    let scores = [95, 70, 88, 80];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let names = [\"Ann\", \"Bob\", \"Vicky\", \"Greg\"];\n    let scores = [95, 70, 88, 80];\n    for (n, s) in names.iter().zip(scores.iter()).filter(|(_, s)| **s > 80) {\n        println!(\"{n} — {s}\");\n    }\n}","expect":"Ann — 95\nVicky — 88","hints":["`names.iter().zip(scores.iter())` gives pairs `(&name, &score)`.","`filter` gets a reference to the pair, so `**s > 80`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Turn the string `\"3,8,x,1\"` into the sum of its numbers, **skipping** whatever isn't a number. Use `filter_map`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let line = \"3,8,x,1\";\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let line = \"3,8,x,1\";\n    let sum: i32 = line.split(',').filter_map(|p| p.parse::<i32>().ok()).sum();\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","expect":"12","hints":["`filter_map` keeps only the `Some`s. And `.ok()` turns a `Result` into an `Option`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Use `fold` to find the minimum and the maximum of the list at the same time and print `min 2, max 9`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let v = [5, 2, 9, 4];\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let v = [5, 2, 9, 4];\n    let (lo, hi) = v.iter().fold((i32::MAX, i32::MIN), |(lo, hi), &x| (lo.min(x), hi.max(x)));\n    println!(\"min {lo}, max {hi}\");\n}","expect":"min 2, max 9","hints":["The accumulated value can be a pair: `(minimum, maximum)`.","Start with `(i32::MAX, i32::MIN)`, and on each step `(lo.min(x), hi.max(x))`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Your own iterator — just `next`.","`zip` + `filter`, `filter_map` + `ok()`, `fold` with a pair are common tricks."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Write your own iterator and a couple of pipelines"]}]},{"id":"a03","title":"Closures, deeper","sub":"Fn, FnMut, FnOnce, move, and functions that return functions","lessons":[{"id":"a03-1","title":"Three kinds of closures","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A closure takes something from around it","md":"A closure can use variables from outside. **How** it uses them decides its kind:\n\n- `Fn` — only **looks**. You can call it as often as you like.\n- `FnMut` — **changes** things. Can also be called many times, but the closure itself must be `mut`.\n- `FnOnce` — **takes** things for itself (eats them). It can be called only once.\n\n> **Analogy:** a guest in your home. `Fn` looks at the paintings. `FnMut` moves the furniture around. `FnOnce` takes the cake home. You can't take the cake twice."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Ann\");\n\n    // Fn: only reads name\n    let greet = || println!(\"Hi, {name}\");\n    greet();\n    greet();\n\n    // FnMut: changes count\n    let mut count = 0;\n    let mut tick = || count += 1;\n    tick();\n    tick();\n    println!(\"ticks: {count}\");\n\n    // FnOnce: takes name and hands it out\n    let take = || name;\n    let got = take();\n    println!(\"taken: {got}\");\n}","out":"Hi, Ann\nHi, Ann\nticks: 2\ntaken: Ann","after":"Try calling `take()` a second time. The compiler will say the closure has already been \"eaten\"."},{"t":"text","title":"They are also requirements","md":"`Fn`, `FnMut`, `FnOnce` are traits. A function can say **which** closure it accepts:\n\n```\nfn twice<F: Fn()>(f: F) { f(); f(); }\nfn twice_mut<F: FnMut()>(mut f: F) { f(); f(); }\nfn once<F: FnOnce()>(f: F) { f(); }\n```\n- `FnOnce` is the loosest requirement: **any** closure fits.\n- `Fn` is the strictest: only a \"looking\" closure fits."},{"t":"quiz","md":"The closure `|| v.push(1)`, where `v` is a vector outside. Which kind is it?","options":["`Fn`","`FnMut`","`FnOnce`"],"answer":1,"why":["`push` changes the vector, looking is not enough.","Right. It changes `v`.","It doesn't take `v`, it only changes it."]},{"t":"quiz","md":"A function asks for `F: FnOnce()`. Can you pass it an `Fn` closure?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. Something that can be called many times can be called once.","`FnOnce` is the loosest requirement, anything fits."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `apply_n` must call the closure `n` times. Pick the right requirement so the counter works.","code":"fn apply_n<F: Fn()>(n: u32, f: F) {\n    for _ in 0..n {\n        f();\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut total = 0;\n    apply_n(4, || total += 5);\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","solution":"fn apply_n<F: FnMut()>(n: u32, mut f: F) {\n    for _ in 0..n {\n        f();\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut total = 0;\n    apply_n(4, || total += 5);\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","expect":"20","hints":["The closure changes `total`. So it's `FnMut`.","Don't forget `mut f: F` — an `FnMut` can only be called through a mutable variable."]},{"t":"deep","title":"How the compiler picks the kind","md":"The compiler looks at what the closure does with each captured variable and picks the **gentlest** way to capture it:\n- only reads — captures by `&`;\n- changes — by `&mut`;\n- hands it out or passes it to someone by value — takes it.\n\nThe resulting closure implements several traits at once. Every `Fn` is also an `FnMut`, and every `FnMut` is also an `FnOnce`. Like nesting dolls: `Fn` ⊂ `FnMut` ⊂ `FnOnce`.\n\nUnder the hood a closure is a nameless struct. Its fields are the captured variables (or references to them). And calling it is a method on that struct."},{"t":"recap","items":["`Fn` looks, `FnMut` changes, `FnOnce` takes.","As a requirement: `FnOnce` accepts everything, `Fn` is the strictest.","Call an `FnMut` through a `mut` variable."]}],"goals":["See how `Fn`, `FnMut` and `FnOnce` differ","Guess which one you'll get"]},{"id":"a03-2","title":"move: take it with you","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"What move does","md":"Normally a closure **borrows** outside variables. `move` makes it **take** them (move them inside).\n\n```\nlet name = String::from(\"Bob\");\nlet f = move || println!(\"{name}\");\n// name is gone here: it's inside f\n```\nNote: `move` changes how things are **captured**, but doesn't necessarily make the closure `FnOnce`. This `f` still only reads `name`, so it's `Fn`, and you can call it many times."},{"t":"text","title":"Where move is needed","md":"When the closure **lives longer** than the variables around it:\n- it's handed to another thread (`thread::spawn`);\n- it's returned from a function;\n- it's put into a struct that will travel far.\n\nWithout `move`, the closure would hold references to variables that are about to disappear. Rust won't allow that.\n\n> **Analogy:** leaving home for a long time, you take your things with you, not a note saying \"umbrella's in the closet\"."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let data = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    let handle = thread::spawn(move || {\n        let sum: i32 = data.iter().sum();\n        println!(\"sum in the thread: {sum}\");\n    });\n    handle.join().unwrap();\n}","out":"sum in the thread: 6","after":"Remove `move` and run it. The compiler will say the thread may outlive `data`."},{"t":"text","title":"move and copyable types","md":"If the captured variable is `Copy` (numbers, `bool`, `char`), `move` simply puts a **copy** inside. The outside variable stays.\n\n```\nlet x = 5;\nlet f = move || x + 1;\nprintln!(\"{x} {}\", f()); // 5 6 — x is alive\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Do you need `move` in `thread::spawn(|| println!(\"{v:?}\"))`, where `v` is a vector from `main`?","options":["Yes","No, `println!` doesn't change anything","Only if `v` is empty"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. The thread may live longer than `main`, so it needs its own data.","It's not about changing, it's about lifetime.","Whether the vector is empty doesn't matter."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`move` — take the captured things into the closure.","Needed when the closure outlives its surroundings: threads, returning from a function.","`Copy` values are copied by `move`."]}],"goals":["See why you write `move` before a closure","Know where `move` is required"]},{"id":"a03-3","title":"Return a closure","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A function factory","md":"A function can **create** and return a closure:\n\n```\nfn make_adder(n: i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    move |x| x + n\n}\n```\n`make_adder(5)` is a new function \"add 5\".\n\n> **Analogy:** a rubber stamp. You set it once (\"5\"), then stamp as often as you like."},{"t":"code","code":"fn make_adder(n: i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    move |x| x + n\n}\n\nfn make_greeter(greeting: &str) -> impl Fn(&str) -> String {\n    let g = greeting.to_string();\n    move |name| format!(\"{g}, {name}!\")\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let add5 = make_adder(5);\n    let add10 = make_adder(10);\n    println!(\"{} {}\", add5(1), add10(1));\n\n    let hi = make_greeter(\"Hello\");\n    println!(\"{}\", hi(\"Vicky\"));\n}","out":"6 11\nHello, Vicky!","after":"`move` is required here: `n` and `g` die when the function ends, but the closure keeps living."},{"t":"text","title":"When you need Box<dyn Fn>","md":"`impl Fn` means \"one concrete type\". What if a function returns **different** closures in different branches?\n\n```\nfn op(name: &str) -> Box<dyn Fn(i32, i32) -> i32> {\n    match name {\n        \"+\" => Box::new(|a, b| a + b),\n        \"*\" => Box::new(|a, b| a * b),\n        _ => Box::new(|_, _| 0),\n    }\n}\n```\nEach closure has its own type, even if they look alike. So — a box with `dyn`, like in the traits module."},{"t":"code","code":"fn op(name: &str) -> Box<dyn Fn(i32, i32) -> i32> {\n    match name {\n        \"+\" => Box::new(|a, b| a + b),\n        \"*\" => Box::new(|a, b| a * b),\n        _ => Box::new(|_, _| 0),\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for name in [\"+\", \"*\", \"?\"] {\n        let f = op(name);\n        println!(\"6 {name} 7 = {}\", f(6, 7));\n    }\n}","out":"6 + 7 = 13\n6 * 7 = 42\n6 ? 7 = 0"},{"t":"quiz","md":"A function returns `|x| x + 1` in one `if` branch and `|x| x * 2` in the other. What goes in the return type?","options":["`impl Fn(i32) -> i32`","`Box<dyn Fn(i32) -> i32>`","`Fn(i32) -> i32`"],"answer":1,"why":["These are two different types, `impl` can't unite them.","Right.","You can't write it without `impl` or `dyn`."]},{"t":"text","title":"Plain functions fit too","md":"The type `fn(i32) -> i32` (lowercase `f`) is a **function pointer**. It's a plain function, or a closure that captures nothing.\n\n```\nfn double(x: i32) -> i32 { x * 2 }\nlet f: fn(i32) -> i32 = double;\nlet v: Vec<i32> = [1, 2].iter().map(|&x| f(x)).collect();\n```\nA plain function can go anywhere an `Fn` is expected: `.map(double)` works too."},{"t":"task","md":"Write `make_multiplier(k)`, which returns a closure \"multiply by k\".","code":"fn make_multiplier(k: i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let triple = make_multiplier(3);\n    println!(\"{}\", triple(7));\n    let v: Vec<i32> = vec![1, 2, 3].into_iter().map(make_multiplier(10)).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"fn make_multiplier(k: i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    move |x| x * k\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let triple = make_multiplier(3);\n    println!(\"{}\", triple(7));\n    let v: Vec<i32> = vec![1, 2, 3].into_iter().map(make_multiplier(10)).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"21\n[10, 20, 30]","hints":["The body is one line: `move |x| x * k`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`-> impl Fn(A) -> B` — return one closure. Almost always with `move`.","Different closures in different branches — `Box<dyn Fn(A) -> B>`.","`fn(A) -> B` — a pointer to a plain function."]}],"goals":["Write a function that returns a function","Choose between `impl Fn` and `Box<dyn Fn>`"]},{"id":"a03-4","title":"Closures in structs","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A function field","md":"You can put a closure into a struct. Two ways:\n\n```\nstruct Button<F: Fn()> {\n    on_click: F,                 // one concrete type, fast\n}\n\nstruct Menu {\n    actions: Vec<Box<dyn Fn()>>, // many different closures\n}\n```\nThe second way is for when there are many handlers and they differ."},{"t":"code","code":"struct EventBus {\n    handlers: Vec<Box<dyn Fn(&str)>>,\n}\n\nimpl EventBus {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        EventBus { handlers: Vec::new() }\n    }\n    fn subscribe(&mut self, f: impl Fn(&str) + 'static) {\n        self.handlers.push(Box::new(f));\n    }\n    fn emit(&self, event: &str) {\n        for h in &self.handlers {\n            h(event);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut bus = EventBus::new();\n    bus.subscribe(|e| println!(\"log: {e}\"));\n    let prefix = String::from(\">>\");\n    bus.subscribe(move |e| println!(\"{prefix} {}\", e.to_uppercase()));\n    bus.emit(\"save\");\n}","out":"log: save\n>> SAVE","after":"`+ 'static` means \"the closure holds no references to temporary things\". That's why `prefix` needs `move`."},{"t":"text","title":"Calling a field","md":"If a closure sits in a field, the call goes in parentheses:\n\n```\n(self.on_click)();\n```\nWithout them, `self.on_click()` makes Rust look for a **method** `on_click`, not the field."},{"t":"fill","explain":"`(self.on_click)()` — first get the field, then call it.","md":"Fill in the blanks so the button calls its handler.","code":"struct Button<F: Fn()> {\n    on_click: F,\n}\n\nimpl<F: Fn()> Button<F> {\n    fn press(&self) {\n        ___self.on_click___();\n    }\n}","answers":[["("],[")"]]},{"t":"task","md":"Make a `Counter` that stores its step as a closure `Box<dyn Fn(i32) -> i32>` and applies it in the `next` method. Start at 1, the step multiplies by 3. Print three values.","code":"struct Counter {\n    value: i32,\n    step: Box<dyn Fn(i32) -> i32>,\n}\n\nimpl Counter {\n    fn next(&mut self) -> i32 {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = Counter { value: 1, step: Box::new(|x| x * 3) };\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n}","solution":"struct Counter {\n    value: i32,\n    step: Box<dyn Fn(i32) -> i32>,\n}\n\nimpl Counter {\n    fn next(&mut self) -> i32 {\n        self.value = (self.step)(self.value);\n        self.value\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = Counter { value: 1, step: Box::new(|x| x * 3) };\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n    println!(\"{}\", c.next());\n}","expect":"3\n9\n27","hints":["Calling a field goes in parentheses: `(self.step)(self.value)`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A closure in a field: `F: Fn()` (one type) or `Box<dyn Fn()>` (different ones).","Call a field: `(self.f)(args)`.","`+ 'static` — the closure holds no temporary references."]}],"goals":["Store a closure in a struct field","Build a simple event handler system"]},{"id":"a03-p","title":"Practice: closures","min":14,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"The function `compose(f, g)` returns a new function \"first `f`, then `g`\". Finish it.","code":"fn compose(f: impl Fn(i32) -> i32, g: impl Fn(i32) -> i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let inc_then_double = compose(|x| x + 1, |x| x * 2);\n    println!(\"{}\", inc_then_double(5));\n}","solution":"fn compose(f: impl Fn(i32) -> i32, g: impl Fn(i32) -> i32) -> impl Fn(i32) -> i32 {\n    move |x| g(f(x))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let inc_then_double = compose(|x| x + 1, |x| x * 2);\n    println!(\"{}\", inc_then_double(5));\n}","expect":"12","hints":["The new closure takes `f` and `g` with it: `move |x| ...`.","First `f(x)`, then pass the result to `g`: `g(f(x))`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `run_once` takes an `FnOnce() -> String` and prints the result. Pass it a closure that **hands out** the `report` string.","code":"fn run_once<F: FnOnce() -> String>(f: F) {\n    println!(\"{}\", f());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let report = String::from(\"report ready\");\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn run_once<F: FnOnce() -> String>(f: F) {\n    println!(\"{}\", f());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let report = String::from(\"report ready\");\n    run_once(move || report);\n}","expect":"report ready","hints":["The closure just returns `report`: `|| report`. You can add `move` for clarity."]},{"t":"task","md":"Build a \"calculator\": a `Vec<(&str, Box<dyn Fn(i32, i32) -> i32>)>` with the operations `+`, `-`, `max`. Apply each to 8 and 3 and print `name: result`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let a = 8;\n    let b = 3;\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let a = 8;\n    let b = 3;\n    let ops: Vec<(&str, Box<dyn Fn(i32, i32) -> i32>)> = vec![\n        (\"+\", Box::new(|x, y| x + y)),\n        (\"-\", Box::new(|x, y| x - y)),\n        (\"max\", Box::new(|x: i32, y| x.max(y))),\n    ];\n    for (name, f) in &ops {\n        println!(\"{name}: {}\", f(a, b));\n    }\n}","expect":"+: 11\n-: 5\nmax: 8","hints":["Each item is a pair `(\"name\", Box::new(|x, y| ...))`.","For `max` the compiler may need a type: `|x: i32, y| x.max(y)`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `memo_square` returns an `FnMut(u64)` closure that computes a square and **counts its calls**. Print the result and the call count after three calls.","code":"fn memo_square() -> impl FnMut(u64) -> (u64, u32) {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut sq = memo_square();\n    sq(2);\n    sq(3);\n    let (v, calls) = sq(4);\n    println!(\"{v} {calls}\");\n}","solution":"fn memo_square() -> impl FnMut(u64) -> (u64, u32) {\n    let mut calls = 0;\n    move |x| {\n        calls += 1;\n        (x * x, calls)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut sq = memo_square();\n    sq(2);\n    sq(3);\n    let (v, calls) = sq(4);\n    println!(\"{v} {calls}\");\n}","expect":"16 3","hints":["Make `let mut calls = 0;` and return `move |x| { calls += 1; (x * x, calls) }`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Combine functions — return `move |x| g(f(x))`.","A closure with its own state — `FnMut` + `move`.","A list of different operations — `Vec<Box<dyn Fn(...)>>`."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Use all three kinds of closures and return a closure from a function"]}]}],"glossary":[]};
